// Chapter headings and background written for Joined: Genesis and Exodus.
Object.assign(CHAPTERS,{
"gen.1":{title:"Let there be light",year:"The Creation",
 sum:"In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth—Light, firmament, land, seas and plants come forth—Sun, moon, stars, fish, birds and beasts are made—God creates man, male and female, in his own image and gives them dominion—Everything he made was very good.",
 bg:["Moses 2 and Abraham 4 retell this chapter. Moses 2:26 shows God speaking to his Only Begotten, and Abraham 4:1 says “the Gods” organized and formed the heavens and the earth.","The Hebrew verb for “created” (bara) is used in the Bible only with God as the one who creates. The days follow a clear pattern, with forming in the first three and filling in the last three."]},
"gen.2":{title:"It is not good that the man should be alone",year:"The Creation",
 sum:"God rests on the seventh day and sanctifies it—The LORD God forms man of the dust and breathes into him the breath of life—He plants a garden in Eden and forbids the tree of the knowledge of good and evil—The woman is made from the man’s rib—They shall be one flesh.",
 bg:["Moses 3 and Abraham 5 parallel this chapter. Moses 3:5 adds that the Lord created all things spiritually before they were naturally upon the earth.","The name Adam is linked to adamah, Hebrew for “ground,” since man was formed from the dust (2:7). “Help meet” translates ezer kenegdo, “a helper fit for him”; the same word ezer is often used of God helping Israel."]},
"gen.3":{title:"Where art thou?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The serpent beguiles Eve, and she and Adam eat the forbidden fruit—Their eyes are opened and they hide from the LORD God—God asks, Where art thou?—The serpent is cursed, and the woman’s seed shall bruise his head—Adam and Eve receive coats of skins and are sent out of Eden, and cherubims guard the tree of life.",
 bg:["Moses 4 parallels this chapter and adds Satan’s rebellion in the premortal council (Moses 4:1–4). The Book of Mormon teaches that “Adam fell that men might be; and men are, that they might have joy” (2 Nephi 2:25), and Moses 5:10–11 records Adam and Eve rejoicing in the plan of redemption.","Adam names his wife Eve “because she was the mother of all living” (3:20); her Hebrew name Chavah sounds like the word for “living.” Christians have long read 3:15, where the woman’s seed bruises the serpent’s head, as the first promise of a Redeemer."]},
"gen.4":{title:"Am I my brother’s keeper?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Cain and Abel are born—The LORD respects Abel’s offering but not Cain’s, and warns that sin lieth at the door—Cain slays Abel and asks, Am I my brother’s keeper?—Cain is driven out as a fugitive with a mark upon him—Seth is born, and men begin to call upon the name of the LORD.",
 bg:["Moses 5 greatly expands this chapter. It tells of an angel teaching Adam that his sacrifices are “a similitude of the sacrifice of the Only Begotten” (Moses 5:7), and of Cain loving Satan more than God (Moses 5:18).","Eve explains Cain’s name with “I have gotten a man” (4:1), a play on the Hebrew qanah, “to get.” Seth’s name sounds like the word for “appointed,” because God “appointed” her another seed in place of Abel (4:25)."]},
"gen.5":{title:"Enoch walked with God",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The book of the generations of Adam, made in the likeness of God—The line runs from Adam through Seth to Lamech—Enoch walked with God, and he was not, for God took him—Methuselah lives 969 years—Lamech names his son Noah, and Noah begets Shem, Ham and Japheth.",
 bg:["Moses 6–7 tell much more about Enoch, including his preaching and the city of Zion that was taken up to heaven (Moses 7:18–21, 69). Moses 8:1–12 parallels the end of this chapter.","Lamech names his son Noah, saying, “This same shall comfort us” (5:29), a play on the Hebrew word nacham, “to comfort.” Scholars read the very long lifespans in this chapter in different ways."]},
"gen.6":{title:"Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The sons of God take wives of the daughters of men, and there are giants in the earth—Wickedness is great, and every imagination of man’s heart is only evil—It repents the LORD that he made man—But Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD—Noah is told to build an ark of gopher wood and bring in his family and two of every living thing.",
 bg:["Moses 8 parallels the first part of this chapter and adds that Noah preached, “Believe and repent of your sins and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ” (Moses 8:24), but the people would not listen.","In Hebrew, Noah’s name (n-ch) spelled backward is chen (ch-n), “grace,” so “Noah found grace” (6:8) is a wordplay many commentators note. A cubit was about 18 inches, making the ark roughly 450 feet long, 75 feet wide and 45 feet high."]},
"gen.7":{title:"The windows of heaven were opened",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Noah and his house enter the ark, with clean beasts by sevens and others by twos—In Noah’s six hundredth year the fountains of the great deep are broken up and the windows of heaven are opened—It rains forty days and forty nights, and the LORD shuts him in—Every living thing on the earth dies—The waters prevail 150 days.",
 bg:["“Fountains of the great deep” and “windows of heaven” (7:11) reflect the ancient picture of waters above the sky and below the earth (1:7). The flood undoes the separation of waters made at the Creation.","Other ancient peoples in Mesopotamia also told flood stories, such as the Epic of Gilgamesh and the Atrahasis account. Scholars compare and debate how these accounts relate to Genesis."]},
"gen.8":{title:"And God remembered Noah",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"God remembers Noah, and the waters go down—The ark rests upon the mountains of Ararat—Noah sends forth a raven and a dove, and the dove returns with an olive leaf—Noah leaves the ark and builds an altar unto the LORD—While the earth remaineth, seedtime and harvest shall not cease.",
 bg:["Ararat is the Hebrew name for the land known in Assyrian records as Urartu, around Lake Van in eastern Turkey and Armenia. The text says “the mountains of Ararat,” not one particular peak.","Noah’s altar (8:20) is the first altar named in the Bible. His first act on dry ground was to worship and give thanks."]},
"gen.9":{title:"I do set my bow in the cloud",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"God blesses Noah and his sons to multiply and replenish the earth—They may eat meat, but not with the blood—Whoso sheddeth man’s blood, by man shall his blood be shed, for man is made in God’s image—God sets his bow in the cloud as a token of his covenant—Noah drinks wine, Canaan is cursed and Shem and Japheth are blessed.",
 bg:["The Joseph Smith Translation adds that when Noah’s seed keep the commandments, Zion will come again, and God will remember the covenant he made with Enoch when he looks upon the bow (JST Genesis 9:21–25).","Human life is sacred because “in the image of God made he man” (9:6). The curse in 9:25 falls on Canaan, Ham’s son, and ancient readers connected it with Israel’s later dealings with the Canaanites."]},
"gen.10":{title:"The nations divided in the earth after the flood",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The generations of the sons of Noah—The descendants of Japheth spread to the isles of the Gentiles—Ham’s line includes Cush, Egypt and Canaan, and Nimrod, a mighty hunter, rules Babel and builds Nineveh—The sons of Shem include Eber and Peleg, in whose days the earth was divided.",
 bg:["This list is often called the Table of Nations and is usually counted as seventy names. Many can be matched with known peoples, such as Mizraim (Egypt), Javan (the Greeks) and Asshur (Assyria). The word “Semitic” comes from Shem.","Peleg means “division,” “for in his days was the earth divided” (10:25). Readers have long disagreed about whether this refers to peoples, languages or the land itself."]},
"gen.11":{title:"Let us build us a city and a tower",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The whole earth has one language—Men build a city and a tower whose top may reach unto heaven, to make them a name—The LORD confounds their language and scatters them, and the place is called Babel—The generations from Shem to Terah—Terah takes Abram, Sarai and Lot from Ur of the Chaldees to Haran, where he dies.",
 bg:["The tower was likely a ziggurat, a stepped temple tower of baked brick, with bitumen (“slime”) for mortar (11:3). The name Babel sounds like the Hebrew balal, “to confound” (11:9). The Book of Mormon tells how the brother of Jared and his people were spared from having their language confounded (Ether 1:33–37).","Abraham 1–2 adds to this chapter, telling how Abraham’s fathers turned to idolatry in Ur, how he sought “the blessings of the fathers” (Abraham 1:2), and how the Lord delivered him. Scholars disagree about where Ur of the Chaldees was located."]},
"gen.12":{title:"In thee shall all families of the earth be blessed",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"The LORD calls Abram to leave his country, promising to make of him a great nation and to bless all families of the earth through him—Abram, 75 years old, departs with Sarai and Lot—He builds altars at Shechem and near Bethel—In a famine Abram goes to Egypt and calls Sarai his sister—Pharaoh is plagued and sends them away.",
 bg:["Abraham 2 expands these promises: through Abraham’s seed the gospel and priesthood would bless all the families of the earth (Abraham 2:9–11). Abraham 2:22–25 adds that the Lord told Abraham to say Sarai was his sister to save his life.","Shechem lay in the hill country of central Canaan, and Bethel, meaning “house of God,” about 10 miles north of Jerusalem. Abram marked his new land not with cities but with altars."]},
"gen.13":{title:"Let there be no strife",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Abram, very rich, returns to the altar at Bethel—His herdmen and Lot’s herdmen strive—Abram says, Let there be no strife … for we be brethren, and gives Lot first choice—Lot chooses the plain of Jordan and pitches his tent toward Sodom—The LORD promises Abram the land and seed as the dust of the earth, and Abram dwells at Mamre in Hebron.",
 bg:["Though Abram was the elder and the head of the family, he let Lot choose first. Lot chose by what looked best, “like the garden of the LORD” (13:10), and moved toward a city whose men “were wicked and sinners before the LORD exceedingly” (13:13).","Hebron lies in the hills about 20 miles south of Jerusalem. Mamre, named for a local ally (14:13), became Abraham’s home base for many years."]},
"gen.14":{title:"Melchizedek king of Salem",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Four kings make war on the kings of Sodom and the plain and carry Lot away—Abram the Hebrew pursues with 318 trained servants and rescues Lot—Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of the most high God, brings bread and wine and blesses Abram—Abram gives him tithes of all—Abram refuses the spoils of Sodom.",
 bg:["The Joseph Smith Translation adds much about Melchizedek, telling of his faith, his priesthood after the order of the Son of God, and how his people “wrought righteousness” and obtained heaven (JST Genesis 14:25–40). Alma 13:14–19 and Doctrine and Covenants 107:2–4 also teach about him, and the higher priesthood bears his name.","Melchizedek means “king of righteousness” (Hebrews 7:2). Salem is traditionally identified with Jerusalem (Psalm 76:2). This chapter is the first place the Bible calls Abram “the Hebrew” (14:13)."]},
"gen.15":{title:"Tell the stars",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Fear not, Abram: I am thy shield—Abram asks for an heir, and the LORD tells him to number the stars, so shall thy seed be—He believed in the LORD, and it was counted to him for righteousness—The LORD makes a covenant with Abram over divided animals—His seed will be afflicted 400 years and then come out with great substance.",
 bg:["Paul quotes 15:6 to teach that Abraham was made righteous through faith (Romans 4:3). Abraham 3:14 also records the Lord promising Abraham seed as numerous as the stars.","Hebrew speaks of “cutting” a covenant. Walking between divided animals was an ancient way of sealing an oath, as Jeremiah 34:18 shows. Here only the smoking furnace and burning lamp, representing God, pass between the pieces."]},
"gen.16":{title:"Thou God seest me",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Sarai, having no children, gives her Egyptian handmaid Hagar to Abram—Hagar conceives and despises Sarai, and flees when Sarai deals hardly with her—The angel of the LORD finds Hagar by a fountain and tells her to return—Her son will be called Ishmael—Hagar calls the LORD “Thou God seest me.”",
 bg:["Ancient marriage contracts from the Near East show that a childless wife could give a servant to her husband to bear children for her. Doctrine and Covenants 132:34 says God commanded this, and Sarah gave Hagar to Abraham.","Ishmael means “God hears,” “because the LORD hath heard thy affliction” (16:11). Hagar named the well Beer-lahai-roi, “the well of him that liveth and seeth me” (16:13–14)."]},
"gen.17":{title:"Walk before me, and be thou perfect",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"The Almighty God appears to Abram at ninety-nine and says, Walk before me, and be thou perfect—Abram’s name becomes Abraham, a father of many nations—The everlasting covenant includes the land of Canaan—Circumcision is the token of the covenant—Sarai becomes Sarah and is promised a son, Isaac, and Ishmael is also blessed.",
 bg:["Abram means “exalted father,” and Abraham is explained as “a father of many nations” (17:5). “Almighty God” translates El Shaddai, a name Exodus 6:3 says God used with the patriarchs.","The Joseph Smith Translation adds that children are not accountable before God until they are eight years old (JST Genesis 17:11). Circumcision was done on the eighth day (17:12)."]},
"gen.18":{title:"Is any thing too hard for the LORD?",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Three men visit Abraham at Mamre, and he hurries to serve them—Sarah will have a son, and she laughs—Is any thing too hard for the LORD?—The LORD tells Abraham of the cry of Sodom and Gomorrah—Abraham pleads for the city from fifty righteous down to ten: Shall not the Judge of all the earth do right?",
 bg:["Abraham’s generous welcome to strangers became a model of hospitality. Hebrews 13:2 likely recalls it: “some have entertained angels unawares.”","Abraham’s bargaining shows a man who knew God well enough to plead boldly for others. The Lord agreed each time, even for the sake of ten righteous people (18:32)."]},
"gen.19":{title:"Escape for thy life",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Two angels come to Sodom, and Lot takes them in, and the men of the city who surround the house are struck with blindness—Lot warns his sons-in-law, but he seems to them as one that mocked—Escape for thy life; look not behind thee—Fire and brimstone fall, and Lot’s wife becomes a pillar of salt—Moab and Ammon are born to Lot’s daughters.",
 bg:["Ezekiel 16:49–50 adds that Sodom’s sins included pride, idleness and refusing to help the poor and needy. Jesus warned, “Remember Lot’s wife” (Luke 17:32). The exact site of Sodom near the Dead Sea is still debated.","The Moabites and Ammonites lived east of the Jordan River and the Dead Sea. Ruth, an ancestor of King David, was a Moabite (Ruth 1:4; 4:17)."]},
"gen.20":{title:"He is a prophet",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"In Gerar, Abraham says of Sarah, She is my sister, and King Abimelech takes her—God warns Abimelech in a dream that Abraham is a prophet who will pray for him—Abimelech restores Sarah with gifts—Abraham prays, and God heals Abimelech’s household.",
 bg:["This is the first time the Bible uses the word “prophet” (20:7). Abraham explains that Sarah was in fact his half sister, the daughter of his father but not of his mother (20:12).","Gerar was in the dry south of Canaan, near Gaza. Abraham feared that “the fear of God is not in this place” (20:11), yet Abimelech proved honest when God warned him."]},
"gen.21":{title:"God hath made me to laugh",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Isaac is born when Abraham is a hundred years old—Sarah says, God hath made me to laugh—Hagar and Ishmael are sent away, and God shows Hagar a well in the wilderness—Ishmael grows up an archer in Paran—Abraham and Abimelech make a covenant at Beer-sheba.",
 bg:["Isaac means “he laughs.” Sarah’s doubting laughter (18:12) turns to joy: “God hath made me to laugh, so that all that hear will laugh with me” (21:6). Paul later uses Sarah and Hagar as an allegory of two covenants (Galatians 4:22–31).","Beer-sheba means “well of the oath” or “well of seven,” recalling the seven ewe lambs and the oath sworn there (21:28–31). It later marked the southern edge of Israel’s land, as in “from Dan even to Beer-sheba.”"]},
"gen.22":{title:"God will provide himself a lamb",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"God tests Abraham: take thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and offer him on Moriah—Abraham tells Isaac, God will provide himself a lamb—The angel of the LORD stops him, and a ram caught in a thicket is offered instead—Abraham names the place Jehovah-jireh—In thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed.",
 bg:["The Book of Mormon teaches that this was “a similitude of God and his Only Begotten Son” (Jacob 4:5). Like Isaac, Jesus carried the wood for the sacrifice and was a beloved, only son.","Jehovah-jireh means “the LORD will see” or “the LORD will provide” (22:14). 2 Chronicles 3:1 says Solomon later built the temple on Mount Moriah."]},
"gen.23":{title:"A possession of a buryingplace",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Sarah dies at 127 years in Hebron, and Abraham mourns for her—Abraham, a stranger and sojourner, asks the sons of Heth for a burying place—He buys the field and cave of Machpelah from Ephron the Hittite for four hundred shekels of silver—Abraham buries Sarah there.",
 bg:["The polite bargaining at the city gate, with witnesses present, matches the way land was bought in the ancient Near East. This cave was the only part of the promised land Abraham ever owned.","Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Leah and Jacob were later buried in Machpelah too (49:31; 50:13). The traditional site in Hebron is covered by a large building with walls from the time of Herod the Great."]},
"gen.24":{title:"I will go",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Abraham has his servant swear to find a wife for Isaac from his kindred, not the Canaanites—At a well the servant prays for a sign, and Rebekah offers water to him and his camels—Laban welcomes him, and he tells of the LORD’s guidance—Rebekah says, I will go—Isaac takes Rebekah, loves her and is comforted after his mother’s death.",
 bg:["The servant traveled to Mesopotamia, in Hebrew Aram-naharaim, “Aram of the two rivers” (24:10). Putting a hand under the thigh was a solemn form of oath (also 47:29).","A thirsty camel can drink many gallons of water, so watering ten camels was hard, generous work. Rebekah offered it without being asked, the very sign the servant had prayed for (24:14, 19)."]},
"gen.25":{title:"Sell me this day thy birthright",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Abraham dies at 175 years, and Isaac and Ishmael bury him in Machpelah—The twelve princes of Ishmael—Rebekah conceives twins after Isaac prays, and the LORD says the elder shall serve the younger—Esau is born red and hairy, and Jacob takes hold of his heel—Esau sells his birthright for red pottage.",
 bg:["Esau was “red” and “hairy” (25:25), and he was called Edom, “red,” after the red pottage (25:30). Jacob, who “took hold on Esau’s heel” (25:26), has a name that sounds like the Hebrew word for heel.","The birthright usually brought a double portion of inheritance (Deuteronomy 21:17) and leadership of the family. Hebrews 12:16 warns against being like Esau, who “for one morsel of meat sold his birthright.”"]},
"gen.26":{title:"Fear not, for I am with thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"In a famine the Lord appears to Isaac and renews the covenant of Abraham—Isaac calls Rebekah his sister in Gerar—He reaps an hundredfold, and the Philistines strive with him over his wells—The Lord appears at Beer-sheba: “Fear not, for I am with thee”—Esau’s Hittite wives are “a grief of mind” to his parents.",
 bg:["The well names tell the story: Esek means “contention,” Sitnah means “hostility” or “opposition,” and Rehoboth means “broad places,” because “now the Lord hath made room for us” (26:20–22).","In the dry Negev, a well could decide whether a family survived. Beer-sheba means “well of the oath” or “well of seven,” and Isaac’s servants find water there the same day he makes an oath with Abimelech (26:31–33)."]},
"gen.27":{title:"The voice is Jacob’s voice",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Isaac, old and nearly blind, sends Esau to hunt venison—Rebekah prepares Jacob, covered in goatskins, to receive the blessing—“The voice is Jacob’s voice, but the hands are the hands of Esau”—Esau cries “with a great and exceeding bitter cry” and plans to kill his brother—Rebekah sends Jacob away to Laban.",
 bg:["Before the twins were born, the Lord told Rebekah that “the elder shall serve the younger” (25:23). The name Jacob is tied to the Hebrew word for “heel,” and Esau plays on it, saying he has been “supplanted” twice (27:36).","Isaac will not take back the blessing even after he learns the truth: “yea, and he shall be blessed” (27:33). In the ancient world a father’s spoken blessing was treated as binding."]},
"gen.28":{title:"This is the gate of heaven",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Isaac gives Jacob “the blessing of Abraham” and sends him to Padan-aram for a wife—Esau marries a daughter of Ishmael—Jacob dreams of a ladder reaching to heaven, with angels ascending and descending—The Lord renews the covenant—“Surely the Lord is in this place; and I knew it not”—Jacob names the place Beth-el and vows to give a tenth.",
 bg:["Beth-el means “house of God”; the town was first called Luz (28:19). Latter-day Saints often see Jacob’s ladder, with its covenant promises and the words “house of God” and “gate of heaven,” as a picture of the temple.","Jacob’s promise to give “the tenth” (28:22) is one of the earliest mentions of tithing in the Bible, after Abraham gave tithes to Melchizedek (14:20)."]},
"gen.29":{title:"But a few days, for the love he had to her",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Jacob meets Rachel at a well and weeps for joy—He serves Laban seven years for Rachel, and they seem “but a few days, for the love he had to her”—Laban gives him Leah instead—Jacob serves seven more years for Rachel—The Lord sees that Leah is hated, and she bears Reuben, Simeon, Levi and Judah.",
 bg:["Leah’s son names explain themselves: Reuben (“see, a son”) because “the Lord hath looked upon my affliction,” Simeon (“hearing”), Levi (“joined”) and Judah (“praise”) (29:32–35).","Laban excuses his trick by saying the younger is not given “before the firstborn” (29:26). Many readers notice the irony: Jacob, who took his elder brother’s place, is now deceived about an elder sister."]},
"gen.30":{title:"And God remembered Rachel",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Rachel gives her handmaid Bilhah to Jacob, and Dan and Naphtali are born—Leah gives Zilpah, who bears Gad and Asher—Leah bears Issachar, Zebulun and Dinah—“God remembered Rachel,” and she bears Joseph—Jacob agrees to take the speckled and spotted flocks as his wages, and the Lord prospers him.",
 bg:["Each name is explained: Dan (“judged”), Naphtali (“wrestling”), Gad (“a troop” or “good fortune”), Asher (“happy”), Issachar (“hire”), Zebulun (“dwelling”) and Joseph (“may he add”), for “the Lord shall add to me another son” (30:6–24).","A wife giving her servant to bear children for her, as Sarah did with Hagar (16:2), appears in other ancient Near Eastern marriage records. Mandrakes were a plant thought to help with fertility, but the chapter says it was God who “opened her womb” (30:22)."]},
"gen.31":{title:"The Lord watch between me and thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells Jacob to return to the land of his fathers—Laban had changed his wages ten times, but God did not suffer him to hurt Jacob—Rachel steals her father’s images, and Jacob flees—God warns Laban in a dream, and Laban searches but does not find the images—Jacob and Laban make a covenant at a heap of stones: “The Lord watch between me and thee.”",
 bg:["Laban names the heap Jegar-sahadutha in Aramaic, and Jacob calls it Galeed in Hebrew. Both mean “heap of witness,” and Mizpah means “watchtower” (31:47–49).","The “images” Rachel took were teraphim, small household gods. Some scholars once linked them to inheritance rights using tablets from Nuzi, but many now doubt that view, and why she took them is still debated."]},
"gen.32":{title:"I will not let thee go, except thou bless me",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Angels meet Jacob at Mahanaim—Hearing that Esau comes with four hundred men, Jacob prays, “I am not worthy of the least of all the mercies”—He sends gifts ahead to his brother—Jacob wrestles until the breaking of the day and will not let go without a blessing—His name becomes Israel, and he names the place Peniel.",
 bg:["The text explains Israel as “a prince hast thou power with God and with men, and hast prevailed” (32:28). Peniel means “face of God,” for “I have seen God face to face” (32:30). Mahanaim means “two camps” (32:2).","The Jabbok is a river flowing west into the Jordan, today called the Zarqa in Jordan. Hosea later says Jacob “had power over the angel, and prevailed: he wept, and made supplication unto him” (Hosea 12:4)."]},
"gen.33":{title:"Esau ran to meet him",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Jacob bows seven times as he approaches his brother—Esau runs to meet him, embraces him and kisses him, and they weep—“I have enough, my brother”—Jacob urges him to take the gift, for seeing his face is “as though I had seen the face of God”—Jacob settles near Shechem and builds an altar.",
 bg:["Bowing seven times was a formal sign of respect in that world; letters from Canaanite rulers found at Amarna in Egypt say “seven times and seven times I fall” at the king’s feet. Succoth means “booths” (33:17), and El-elohe-Israel means “God, the God of Israel.”","Jesus’ parable of the prodigal son uses nearly the same words: the father “ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him” (Luke 15:20)."]},
"gen.34":{title:"Dinah the daughter of Leah",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Shechem the son of Hamor takes Dinah and humbles her, then asks to marry her—Jacob’s sons answer deceitfully, requiring the men of the city to be circumcised—Simeon and Levi slay the men of Shechem, and their brothers spoil the city—Jacob says, “Ye have troubled me.”",
 bg:["Shechem was an important city in the hill country of central Canaan, between Mount Ebal and Mount Gerizim, near modern Nablus. Its ruins are at Tell Balata.","Jacob never forgot this violence. In his last words he says of Simeon and Levi, “Cursed be their anger, for it was fierce” (49:5–7)."]},
"gen.35":{title:"Arise, go up to Beth-el",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"Jacob’s household puts away their strange gods and goes up to Beth-el—God appears again, confirms the name Israel and renews the covenant—Rachel dies giving birth to Benjamin and is buried on the way to Bethlehem—The twelve sons of Jacob are named—Isaac dies, and Esau and Jacob bury him.",
 bg:["As she died, Rachel named her son Ben-oni, “son of my sorrow,” but Jacob called him Benjamin, “son of the right hand” (35:18). Allon-bachuth, where Rebekah’s nurse was buried, means “oak of weeping” (35:8).","Jeremiah pictures “Rachel weeping for her children” (Jeremiah 31:15), and Matthew applies it to Herod’s slaughter near Bethlehem (Matthew 2:18). A traditional Rachel’s Tomb stands near Bethlehem, though some scholars place the original site farther north (1 Samuel 10:2)."]},
"gen.36":{title:"Esau, who is Edom",year:"Date uncertain (about 2000 BC)",
 sum:"The generations of Esau, who is Edom—Esau moves to mount Seir, for the land cannot bear both his and Jacob’s cattle—The dukes of Edom and the sons of Seir the Horite—The kings who reigned in Edom before any king reigned over Israel.",
 bg:["Edom means “red” (25:30). The Edomites lived in the rugged land southeast of the Dead Sea, and in New Testament times their descendants, the Idumeans, included Herod the Great.","Amalek, a grandson of Esau (36:12), was the ancestor of the Amalekites, who later attacked Israel in the wilderness (Exodus 17:8)."]},
"gen.37":{title:"Behold, this dreamer cometh",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Israel loves Joseph more than all his children and makes him a coat of many colours—Joseph dreams of sheaves and of the sun, moon and eleven stars bowing to him—His brothers conspire against him: “Behold, this dreamer cometh”—Reuben saves his life, and Judah proposes selling him for twenty pieces of silver—Jacob mourns, believing a beast has devoured him.",
 bg:["The Hebrew behind “coat of many colours” is uncertain; it may mean a long robe with sleeves, the same phrase used for a king’s daughter’s garment (2 Samuel 13:18). In the Book of Mormon, Captain Moroni recalls a remnant of Joseph’s coat that was preserved (Alma 46:23–24).","Dothan lay north of Shechem near a trade route between Gilead and Egypt. Some scholars note that twenty shekels of silver matches slave prices known from the early second millennium BC, though others question how much that proves."]},
"gen.38":{title:"She hath been more righteous than I",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Judah marries a Canaanite and has three sons—Er and Onan die, and Judah withholds his youngest son from Tamar—Tamar disguises herself, and Judah gives her his signet, bracelets and staff as a pledge—Judah confesses, “She hath been more righteous than I”—Tamar bears twins, Pharez and Zarah.",
 bg:["When a man died without children, his brother was expected to marry the widow and raise up a son in the dead man’s name. This custom later appears in the law of Moses (Deuteronomy 25:5–10) and in the book of Ruth.","Pharez, whose name means “breach” (38:29), became an ancestor of Boaz and King David (Ruth 4:18–22). Tamar is one of the women named in Jesus’ genealogy (Matthew 1:3)."]},
"gen.39":{title:"The Lord was with Joseph",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Joseph is sold to Potiphar, and “the Lord was with Joseph”—Potiphar makes him overseer of his house—Joseph refuses Potiphar’s wife: “How then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?”—He flees, leaving his garment, and is falsely accused and cast into prison—The Lord is with him there, and the keeper trusts him with all the prisoners.",
 bg:["Potiphar is a real Egyptian name, probably meaning “he whom Ra gave.” His title, “captain of the guard,” marks him as a high royal official.","The phrase “the Lord was with Joseph” frames the chapter (39:2, 21). Whether Joseph is honored or slandered, the Lord’s presence does not change."]},
"gen.40":{title:"Do not interpretations belong to God?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Pharaoh’s chief butler and chief baker are put in prison with Joseph—Each dreams a dream, and Joseph asks, “Do not interpretations belong to God?”—The butler will be restored in three days and the baker hanged—It happens on Pharaoh’s birthday as Joseph said—“Yet did not the chief butler remember Joseph, but forgat him.”",
 bg:["The chief butler was the king’s cupbearer, a trusted official who served his drink, like Nehemiah later (Nehemiah 1:11). Egyptians took dreams seriously, and dream-interpretation books on papyrus have survived from ancient Egypt.","Joseph uses a play on words: Pharaoh will “lift up thy head” to restore the butler (40:13), but will “lift up thy head from off thee” for the baker (40:19)."]},
"gen.41":{title:"God shall give Pharaoh an answer of peace",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Pharaoh dreams of seven fat and seven lean kine, and of seven good and seven thin ears of corn—The butler remembers Joseph—“It is not in me: God shall give Pharaoh an answer of peace”—Joseph foretells seven years of plenty and seven of famine and is made ruler over Egypt—He gathers corn as the sand of the sea, and Manasseh and Ephraim are born.",
 bg:["Joseph names his sons Manasseh (“making to forget”), for God “hath made me forget all my toil,” and Ephraim (“fruitful”), for “God hath caused me to be fruitful in the land of my affliction” (41:51–52).","Many details fit Egypt: Joseph shaves before seeing Pharaoh, as Egyptian men did, and receives a signet ring, fine linen and a gold chain, as officials do in Egyptian art. On, the home of his wife’s father, is Heliopolis, a center of sun worship near modern Cairo."]},
"gen.42":{title:"We are verily guilty concerning our brother",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Jacob sends ten sons to Egypt to buy corn but keeps Benjamin at home—Joseph’s brothers bow before him, and he knows them, but they know not him—He calls them spies and keeps Simeon bound—The brothers say, “We are verily guilty concerning our brother”—They find their money in their sacks, and Jacob refuses to send Benjamin.",
 bg:["Joseph spoke to his brothers through an interpreter, so they did not know he understood every word of their confession (42:23). He turned away and wept.","In the King James Bible, “corn” means grain, such as wheat and barley, not American maize. Egyptian records and tomb paintings show people from Canaan coming to Egypt in times of need."]},
"gen.43":{title:"God be gracious unto thee, my son",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"The famine is sore, and Judah offers himself as surety for Benjamin—Israel sends a present, double money and Benjamin: “If I be bereaved of my children, I am bereaved”—Simeon is brought out to them—Joseph sees Benjamin, says “God be gracious unto thee, my son,” and weeps in his chamber—The brothers feast, seated by age, and Benjamin’s portion is five times as much.",
 bg:["Egyptians would not eat with Hebrews, “for that is an abomination unto the Egyptians” (43:32), so Joseph ate apart from both his servants and his brothers.","Judah, who once proposed selling Joseph (37:26–27), now says, “I will be surety for him; of my hand shalt thou require him” (43:9). The story is quietly showing how he has changed."]},
"gen.44":{title:"Let thy servant abide instead of the lad",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Joseph has his silver cup hidden in Benjamin’s sack—The steward overtakes the brothers, and the cup is found—They return and fall before Joseph—Judah says, “God hath found out the iniquity of thy servants”—Judah pleads for his father’s sake and offers to stay as a bondman in Benjamin’s place.",
 bg:["The steward calls the cup the one “whereby indeed he divineth” (44:5). Reading signs in liquid in a cup was a known practice in the ancient Near East, and Joseph was playing the part of an Egyptian lord.","Judah’s plea is one of the longest speeches in Genesis. The brother who once sold one son of Rachel now offers his own freedom to save the other."]},
"gen.45":{title:"I am Joseph your brother",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Joseph can no longer refrain himself and says, “I am Joseph”—“God did send me before you to preserve life”—He tells his brothers to bring their father to dwell in Goshen—Pharaoh sends wagons, and Joseph tells them, “See that ye fall not out by the way”—Jacob says, “It is enough; Joseph my son is yet alive.”",
 bg:["Goshen was in the eastern Nile Delta, good pastureland on the side of Egypt nearest Canaan.","Joseph sees God’s hand in his suffering: “it was not you that sent me hither, but God” (45:8). The Lord has taught, “of you it is required to forgive all men” (D&C 64:10)."]},
"gen.46":{title:"Fear not to go down into Egypt",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Israel offers sacrifices at Beer-sheba, and God says, “Fear not to go down into Egypt”—The names of Jacob’s household who came into Egypt, threescore and ten souls—Joseph meets his father in Goshen and weeps on his neck a good while—“Now let me die, since I have seen thy face”—Every shepherd is an abomination unto the Egyptians.",
 bg:["Genesis counts seventy people of Jacob’s family in Egypt (46:27). Stephen says seventy-five (Acts 7:14), following the Greek translation of Genesis, which counts some descendants of Joseph.","Scholars are not sure why Egyptians looked down on shepherds. Whatever the reason, it kept Israel living apart in Goshen, where the family could grow without blending into Egypt."]},
"gen.47":{title:"Few and evil have the days of the years of my life",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Joseph presents five of his brothers to Pharaoh, and they are given the best of the land—Jacob blesses Pharaoh: “few and evil have the days of the years of my life”—As the famine continues, Joseph buys the money, cattle and land of Egypt for Pharaoh—The people give a fifth part to Pharaoh—Jacob makes Joseph swear to bury him with his fathers.",
 bg:["The “land of Rameses” (47:11) uses the name of a later city named for pharaohs called Ramesses, several centuries after Joseph. Many scholars see it as a place name updated for later readers.","Putting a hand under the thigh to swear was a solemn oath, the same one Abraham’s servant took when he went to find a wife for Isaac (24:2–3)."]},
"gen.48":{title:"The Angel which redeemed me from all evil",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Joseph brings Manasseh and Ephraim to his sick father—Jacob adopts them: “as Reuben and Simeon, they shall be mine”—He crosses his hands and puts his right hand on Ephraim, the younger—“The Angel which redeemed me from all evil, bless the lads”—The younger shall be greater, and Joseph receives one portion above his brethren.",
 bg:["By adopting Joseph’s two sons as his own, Jacob gave Joseph a double portion. Chronicles explains that the birthright was given to the sons of Joseph (1 Chronicles 5:1–2).","Jewish parents still bless their sons with Jacob’s words, “God make thee as Ephraim and as Manasseh” (48:20). Latter-day revelation speaks of “the richer blessing upon the head of Ephraim and his fellows” (D&C 133:34)."]},
"gen.49":{title:"Joseph is a fruitful bough",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"Jacob gathers his sons to tell them what shall befall them in the last days—Reuben is unstable as water, and Simeon and Levi are instruments of cruelty—The sceptre shall not depart from Judah until Shiloh come—Joseph is a fruitful bough whose branches run over the wall—Jacob charges them to bury him in the cave of Machpelah and dies.",
 bg:["The meaning of “until Shiloh come” (49:10) is uncertain in Hebrew, and scholars translate it different ways. Judah became the royal tribe of David, and Jews and Christians have long read the verse as pointing to the Messiah.","Latter-day Saints see Joseph’s branches running “over the wall” (49:22) fulfilled in part by Lehi’s family, descendants of Joseph who crossed the sea (1 Nephi 5:14; Alma 10:3)."]},
"gen.50":{title:"God meant it unto good",year:"Date uncertain (about 1700 BC)",
 sum:"The physicians embalm Israel, and Egypt mourns him seventy days—Joseph and a great company bury him in Canaan—His brothers fear revenge, but Joseph says, “ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good”—Joseph dies at an hundred and ten years, saying, “God will surely visit you,” and asks that his bones be carried up from Egypt.",
 bg:["Egyptian embalming involved drying the body with natron salt, and the Greek historian Herodotus said the full process took seventy days. Egyptian texts call 110 years the ideal length of life, the age at which Joseph died.","The Joseph Smith Translation of this chapter adds Joseph’s prophecy of a latter-day seer named Joseph, which is also recorded in 2 Nephi 3. Moses carried Joseph’s bones out of Egypt (Exodus 13:19), and they were buried at Shechem (Joshua 24:32)."]},
"exo.1":{title:"The more they afflicted them, the more they multiplied",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The children of Israel multiply in Egypt—A new king which knew not Joseph sets taskmasters over them, and they build the treasure cities Pithom and Raamses—The midwives Shiphrah and Puah fear God and save the Hebrew boys—Pharaoh commands that every son be cast into the river.",
 bg:["The pharaohs of Exodus are never named, and historians do not know who they were. Raamses was likely Pi-Ramesses, a city in the eastern Nile Delta built in the 1200s BC, one reason some date the Exodus to about 1250 BC; others use 1 Kings 6:1 to date it to about 1450 BC.","Egyptian buildings were made largely of mud brick, and Egyptian tomb paintings show workers making bricks under overseers, much like the scene in Exodus."]},
"exo.2":{title:"Because I drew him out of the water",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A Levite mother hides her son in an ark of bulrushes by the river—Pharaoh’s daughter finds him and names him Moses—Grown, Moses kills an Egyptian and flees to Midian—He marries Zipporah, who bears Gershom—God hears Israel’s groaning and remembers his covenant.",
 bg:["Pharaoh’s daughter named him Moses, “Because I drew him out of the water” (2:10); the name sounds like the Hebrew mashah, “to draw out.” It also resembles the Egyptian word for “son” or “born,” found in names like Thutmose and Ramesses.","The word for the basket, tevah, is used in the Bible only here and for Noah’s ark. Moses named his son Gershom, “for he said, I have been a stranger in a strange land” (2:22)."]},
"exo.3":{title:"The place whereon thou standest is holy ground",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The angel of the Lord appears to Moses in a burning bush that is not consumed—Put off thy shoes, for this is holy ground—The Lord will deliver Israel to a land flowing with milk and honey—God reveals his name: I AM THAT I AM—Israel will not go out empty.",
 bg:["“I AM” is connected to the Hebrew verb “to be,” and is the root of the sacred name Jehovah (Yahweh). Latter-day Saints understand Jehovah to be the premortal Jesus Christ, who said, “Before Abraham was, I am” (John 8:58).","Moses 1 records a vision Moses received after the burning bush, when he saw God face to face and learned he was a son of God (Moses 1:1–6, 17). Horeb is another name for Mount Sinai, whose location is debated."]},
"exo.4":{title:"What is that in thine hand?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord gives Moses signs: a rod that becomes a serpent and a leprous hand made whole—Moses says he is slow of speech, and the Lord appoints Aaron to speak for him—Moses returns toward Egypt—Aaron meets him, and the people believe and worship.",
 bg:["The Joseph Smith Translation of Exodus 4:21 and later verses changes “I will harden his heart” to say that Pharaoh would harden his own heart.","The Lord answered Moses’s fear by asking, “Who hath made man’s mouth?” (4:11). Like many called to serve, Moses felt unequal to the task, and the Lord promised, “I will be with thy mouth.”"]},
"exo.5":{title:"Who is the LORD, that I should obey his voice?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses and Aaron ask Pharaoh to let Israel go to hold a feast in the wilderness—Pharaoh answers, I know not the Lord—He orders the Israelites to gather their own straw yet make the same number of bricks—The Hebrew officers blame Moses, and Moses cries to the Lord.",
 bg:["Straw mixed into mud made bricks stronger and less likely to crack. Gathering stubble from the fields while keeping the same quota made the work far harder.","Pharaoh was considered a god by the Egyptians, so his question, “Who is the LORD?” sets up a contest the plagues will answer."]},
"exo.6":{title:"By my name JEHOVAH",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord promises, I will bring you out, I will redeem you, and I will take you to me for a people—He renews his covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob—Israel hearkens not for anguish of spirit—The genealogy of Reuben, Simeon and Levi, including Moses and Aaron.",
 bg:["“JEHOVAH” in 6:3 is one of the few places the KJV spells out God’s sacred name instead of printing “the LORD.” The Joseph Smith Translation of this verse teaches that the patriarchs did know the name, and the Lord told Abraham, “My name is Jehovah” (Abraham 1:16).","Moses and Aaron were sons of Amram and Jochebed of the tribe of Levi (6:20). The genealogy shows their right to lead and serve."]},
"exo.7":{title:"I have made thee a god to Pharaoh",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses is to be as God to Pharaoh, and Aaron his prophet—Aaron’s rod becomes a serpent and swallows the magicians’ rods—Pharaoh’s heart is hardened—The waters of the river are turned to blood.",
 bg:["The Nile was Egypt’s life. Almost no rain falls there, and nearly all farming and drinking water came from the river, so turning it to blood struck at the heart of the land.","The Lord said the plagues would be judgments “against all the gods of Egypt” (12:12). Egyptians honored the Nile and its yearly flood as gifts of their gods."]},
"exo.8":{title:"This is the finger of God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Frogs cover the land, and Pharaoh promises to let the people go, then hardens his heart—Dust becomes lice, and the magicians say, This is the finger of God—Swarms of flies fill Egypt but not the land of Goshen—Pharaoh again refuses.",
 bg:["The magicians copied the first plagues but could not make lice and admitted a power beyond their own (8:18–19). Jesus used the same phrase when he cast out devils “with the finger of God” (Luke 11:20).","Goshen, in the eastern Nile Delta, was where Jacob’s family had settled (Genesis 47:6). The Lord “put a division” between his people and the Egyptians (8:23)."]},
"exo.9":{title:"That my name may be declared throughout all the earth",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A murrain kills the cattle of Egypt but not of Israel—Ashes become boils on man and beast—The Lord sends thunder, hail and fire that destroy the crops—Pharaoh says, I have sinned, but when the hail stops he hardens his heart again.",
 bg:["Some of Pharaoh’s servants “feared the word of the LORD” and brought their servants and cattle inside before the hail (9:20). Even in Egypt, those who listened were protected.","A murrain is a deadly disease of livestock. Cattle were wealth in Egypt, and some gods were pictured as cattle."]},
"exo.10":{title:"Darkness which may be felt",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Locusts eat every green thing the hail left—Pharaoh offers to let only the men go—Thick darkness covers Egypt for three days, but all Israel has light in their dwellings—Pharaoh threatens Moses that he will see his face no more.",
 bg:["Locust swarms can contain millions of insects and strip fields bare in hours. They have been feared in the Near East throughout history.","Ra, the sun god, was among the most important gods in Egypt. Three days of darkness showed the Lord’s power over what Egyptians worshipped most."]},
"exo.11":{title:"The LORD doth put a difference between the Egyptians and Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord announces one more plague—The Israelites ask the Egyptians for jewels of silver and gold—At midnight all the firstborn of Egypt will die—Against Israel not a dog shall move his tongue—Moses leaves Pharaoh in great anger.",
 bg:["The Lord had promised Moses that Israel would not leave empty-handed (3:21–22). Much of this silver and gold was later given for the tabernacle (35:22).","The firstborn son held a special place as heir in ancient families. Pharaoh had ordered Israel’s sons killed; now judgment would fall on Egypt’s firstborn."]},
"exo.12":{title:"When I see the blood, I will pass over you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord institutes the Passover: a lamb without blemish, its blood on the doorposts, eaten with unleavened bread and bitter herbs—The destroyer passes over the houses marked with blood—At midnight the firstborn of Egypt die—Israel leaves Egypt after 430 years.",
 bg:["The Passover is named for the Lord’s promise, “when I see the blood, I will pass over you” (12:13, 27). Paul wrote, “Christ our passover is sacrificed for us” (1 Corinthians 5:7); like the lamb, not a bone of Jesus was broken (12:46; John 19:36).","Jesus ate the Passover with his apostles at the Last Supper (Luke 22:15). Jews still celebrate Passover each spring, eating unleavened bread to remember the haste of leaving Egypt."]},
"exo.13":{title:"A pillar of a cloud … a pillar of fire",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The firstborn are sanctified to the Lord—Israel is to keep the feast of unleavened bread and teach their children—God leads them away from the land of the Philistines—Moses takes the bones of Joseph—The Lord goes before them in a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night.",
 bg:["The coastal road to Canaan was short but guarded by Egyptian forts. God led Israel another way, “Lest peradventure the people repent when they see war” (13:17).","Joseph had made Israel promise to carry his bones out of Egypt (Genesis 50:25). Keeping that promise showed faith that God would bring them to the promised land."]},
"exo.14":{title:"Stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Pharaoh pursues Israel with chariots to the sea—The people are afraid, and Moses says, Fear ye not, stand still, and see the salvation of the Lord—A strong east wind divides the waters, and Israel walks through on dry ground—The sea returns upon the Egyptians.",
 bg:["The Hebrew name for this sea, yam suph, can mean “sea of reeds.” Its exact location and the route of the Exodus are debated.","Nephi encouraged his brothers with this miracle (1 Nephi 4:2; 17:26–27), and the Lord promised Moses he would be “made stronger than many waters” (Moses 1:25)."]},
"exo.15":{title:"The LORD is my strength and song",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses and Israel sing a song of praise: The Lord hath triumphed gloriously—Miriam the prophetess leads the women with timbrels and dances—At Marah the bitter waters are made sweet—I am the Lord that healeth thee—Israel camps at Elim by twelve wells.",
 bg:["The Song of Moses is one of the oldest poems in the Bible. Revelation 15:3 pictures the faithful singing “the song of Moses … and the song of the Lamb.”","Marah means “bitter” (15:23). The Lord showed Moses a tree that made the water sweet, and promised healing to those who hearken to his voice (15:26)."]},
"exo.16":{title:"It is manna",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"In the wilderness of Sin the people murmur for the flesh pots of Egypt—The Lord sends quails at evening and bread from heaven in the morning—Each gathers an omer a day, and twice as much on the sixth day—No manna falls on the Sabbath—A pot of manna is kept for future generations.",
 bg:["When the Israelites first saw it they said, “It is manna: for they wist not what it was” (16:15); man hu means “What is it?” It was “like coriander seed, white” and tasted “like wafers made with honey” (16:31).","Jesus taught, “I am the bread of life,” comparing himself to the manna Israel ate in the wilderness (John 6:31–35, 48–51)."]},
"exo.17":{title:"Is the LORD among us, or not?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"At Rephidim the people chide Moses for water—The Lord commands Moses to smite the rock in Horeb, and water comes out—Joshua fights Amalek while Aaron and Hur hold up Moses’s hands—Moses builds an altar called Jehovah-nissi.",
 bg:["Moses named the place Massah (“testing”) and Meribah (“quarreling”) because the people tempted the Lord (17:7). Paul wrote of this rock, “and that Rock was Christ” (1 Corinthians 10:4).","Jehovah-nissi means “The LORD is my banner” (17:15). This is the first time Joshua appears in the Bible."]},
"exo.18":{title:"Thou wilt surely wear away",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Jethro brings Zipporah and her sons to Moses and rejoices in what the Lord has done—Moses judges the people from morning to evening—Jethro counsels him to teach the law and choose able men to be rulers of thousands, hundreds, fifties and tens.",
 bg:["Moses received the priesthood from Jethro, the priest of Midian (D&C 84:6). Moses’s second son, Eliezer, was named because “the God of my father … was mine help” (18:4).","Jethro’s counsel to share the load is often used in the Church to teach about delegating and about leaders who teach correct principles."]},
"exo.19":{title:"A kingdom of priests, and an holy nation",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel camps before Mount Sinai in the third month—If they obey and keep the covenant, they will be a peculiar treasure, a kingdom of priests and an holy nation—The people sanctify themselves and wash their clothes—The Lord descends on the mount in fire, smoke and the sound of a trumpet.",
 bg:["Moses “sought diligently to sanctify his people that they might behold the face of God” (D&C 84:23). The preparation here was meant to bring all Israel into the Lord’s presence.","Mount Sinai’s location is debated. Tradition places it at Jebel Musa in the southern Sinai Peninsula, while others propose sites elsewhere, including Arabia."]},
"exo.20":{title:"Thou shalt have no other gods before me",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"God speaks the Ten Commandments—The people fear and ask Moses to speak to them instead—Moses draws near to the thick darkness where God is—Israel is to make no gods of silver or gold, but an altar of earth or unhewn stone.",
 bg:["In Hebrew these are called the “ten words” (34:28). The Book of Mormon prophet Abinadi taught them to King Noah’s priests (Mosiah 12–13).","Jesus summed up the law in two great commandments: love God, and love your neighbor (Matthew 22:37–40). The first commandments concern God; the rest concern others."]},
"exo.21":{title:"Eye for eye, tooth for tooth",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Laws on Hebrew servants, who go free in the seventh year—Penalties for murder, kidnapping and cursing parents—Laws for injuries: eye for eye, tooth for tooth—The owner of an ox that gores is responsible.",
 bg:["“Eye for eye” limited punishment to fit the harm, instead of allowing unlimited revenge. Jesus taught his followers a higher way: to turn the other cheek (Matthew 5:38–39).","Other ancient law codes, such as the Code of Hammurabi of Babylon (about 1750 BC), have similar laws. Israel’s laws stand out for linking justice to a covenant with God."]},
"exo.22":{title:"Ye were strangers in the land of Egypt",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Thieves must restore what they took, and those who damage property must repay—Laws on borrowing, seduction and sorcery—Do not oppress a stranger, widow or fatherless child—Lend to the poor without usury, and return a garment taken in pledge before sundown.",
 bg:["A poor man’s cloak was often also his blanket at night, so the law required it be returned before dark (22:26–27). The Lord says, “I will hear; for I am gracious.”","Israel was to remember their own suffering in Egypt and treat strangers kindly. James later taught that pure religion is to visit the fatherless and widows (James 1:27)."]},
"exo.23":{title:"I send an Angel before thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Be honest in judgment and help even an enemy’s animal—Let the land rest every seventh year, and rest on the seventh day—Keep three feasts each year—An Angel will lead Israel, and the Lord will drive out their enemies little by little.",
 bg:["The three feasts became Passover (Unleavened Bread), the Feast of Weeks or Pentecost (Harvest), and the Feast of Tabernacles (Ingathering). The Holy Ghost came upon the apostles on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:1–4).","The Lord said he would drive out the inhabitants “little by little,” so the land would not become desolate (23:29–30). Blessings often come step by step."]},
"exo.24":{title:"All that the LORD hath said will we do",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses writes the words of the Lord and reads the book of the covenant—The people promise, All that the Lord hath said will we do—Moses sprinkles the blood of the covenant—Moses, Aaron, Nadab, Abihu and seventy elders see the God of Israel—Moses goes up into the cloud forty days and forty nights.",
 bg:["Covenants in the ancient world were often sealed with sacrifice. Jesus echoed Moses’s words at the Last Supper: “this is my blood of the new testament” (Matthew 26:28; see Hebrews 9:19–20).","Moses’s forty days on the mount are recorded in Exodus 25–31, where he received the pattern for the tabernacle."]},
"exo.25":{title:"That I may dwell among them",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord asks for offerings from every willing heart—Let them make me a sanctuary, that I may dwell among them—Patterns for the ark of the covenant, the mercy seat with two cherubims, the table of shewbread and the golden candlestick—Make them after the pattern shewed thee in the mount.",
 bg:["The tabernacle was a portable temple, a pattern for temples ever since. The Lord said he would meet and commune with Moses “from above the mercy seat” (25:22).","A cubit was about 18 inches, so the ark was roughly 45 inches long and 27 inches wide and high. Shittim is acacia, a hard wood that grows in the desert."]},
"exo.26":{title:"Between the holy place and the most holy",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Curtains of fine linen with cherubims, and coverings of goats’ hair and skins, form the tabernacle—Boards of shittim wood overlaid with gold stand in silver sockets—A vail divides the holy place from the most holy, where the ark rests.",
 bg:["Everything was built to be taken down and carried through the wilderness. The most holy place was a perfect cube, about 15 feet each way.","When Jesus died, the veil of the temple was torn in two (Matthew 27:51). Hebrews teaches that through Christ we may enter the holiest “through the veil, that is to say, his flesh” (Hebrews 10:19–20)."]},
"exo.27":{title:"To cause the lamp to burn always",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"An altar of shittim wood overlaid with brass, with horns on its four corners—A court of fine linen hangings, a hundred cubits long, surrounds the tabernacle—Pure olive oil is to keep the lamp burning before the Lord always.",
 bg:["Worshippers entered the court from the east and came first to the altar of sacrifice. The layout taught that coming into God’s presence begins with sacrifice and cleansing, moving step by step toward holier places.","Olive oil was beaten, not pressed with heavy weights, to make it pure and clear for the lamp. Oil is often a symbol of the Holy Spirit."]},
"exo.28":{title:"HOLINESS TO THE LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Holy garments for Aaron and his sons, for glory and for beauty—An ephod with two onyx stones engraved with the names of the tribes—A breastplate of twelve stones, holding the Urim and Thummim over Aaron’s heart—A robe, a mitre and a gold plate engraved HOLINESS TO THE LORD.",
 bg:["The high priest carried Israel’s names on his shoulders and over his heart when he went before the Lord (28:12, 29). Joseph Smith received a Urim and Thummim with the gold plates (Joseph Smith—History 1:35).","The words “Holiness to the Lord” are inscribed on Latter-day Saint temples today."]},
"exo.29":{title:"I will dwell among the children of Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Aaron and his sons are consecrated: washed with water, clothed in holy garments and anointed with oil—Sacrifices are offered, and blood is put on the right ear, thumb and great toe—A lamb is offered morning and evening—The Lord will dwell among Israel and be their God.",
 bg:["Washing, anointing and clothing prepared the priests to serve in God’s house. Latter-day Saints recognize washings and anointings as temple ordinances restored in the latter days (D&C 124:39).","A lamb was offered every morning and evening on the altar (29:38–42), a daily reminder of sacrifice. John the Baptist called Jesus “the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world” (John 1:29)."]},
"exo.30":{title:"To make an atonement for your souls",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A golden altar of incense to burn before the vail morning and evening—Every man gives a half shekel, the rich no more and the poor no less—A laver of brass for washing before service—Holy anointing oil and incense are made for the Lord alone.",
 bg:["Incense rising before the Lord came to symbolize prayer (Psalm 141:2; Revelation 8:3–4).","Everyone paid the same half shekel, showing that each soul is of equal worth to God. Centuries later this became the temple tax mentioned in Matthew 17:24."]},
"exo.31":{title:"I have filled him with the spirit of God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord fills Bezaleel with the Spirit of God to work in gold, silver, brass, stone and wood, with Aholiab to help him—The Sabbath is a sign between the Lord and Israel forever—The Lord gives Moses two tables of stone written with the finger of God.",
 bg:["Skill in craftsmanship is called a gift of the Spirit here: Bezaleel was “filled … with the spirit of God” for his work (31:3). Talents used to build the Lord’s house are a form of worship.","The Sabbath was “a sign … that ye may know that I am the LORD that doth sanctify you” (31:13). Latter-day Saints receive similar counsel in D&C 59:9–13."]},
"exo.32":{title:"Who is on the LORD’s side?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The people tire of waiting, and Aaron makes a golden calf—The Lord’s anger is kindled, and Moses pleads for Israel—Moses breaks the tables, burns the calf and asks, Who is on the Lord’s side?—Moses offers to be blotted out of God’s book for his people.",
 bg:["Bulls were worshipped as symbols of strength in Egypt and Canaan. Centuries later King Jeroboam set up golden calves with the same words, “behold thy gods, O Israel” (1 Kings 12:28).","Moses’s willingness to suffer for his people’s sins (32:32) points toward Jesus Christ, who did suffer for us."]},
"exo.33":{title:"As a man speaketh unto his friend",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord says he will send an angel but not go in Israel’s midst, and the people mourn—Moses meets the Lord in the tabernacle outside the camp, face to face—My presence shall go with thee—Moses asks to see God’s glory and is hidden in a clift of the rock.",
 bg:["Verse 11 says Moses spoke with God face to face, and Moses 1:2 says the same. The Joseph Smith Translation of 33:20 explains that no sinful man could see God’s face “at this time, and live.”","Joshua, Moses’s young servant, “departed not out of the tabernacle” (33:11), an early sign of the leader he would become."]},
"exo.34":{title:"Merciful and gracious, longsuffering",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses hews two new tables of stone—The Lord proclaims his name: merciful, gracious and abundant in goodness and truth—The covenant is renewed with warnings against idols—Moses comes down after forty days, and the skin of his face shines, so he puts a vail on his face.",
 bg:["The Joseph Smith Translation of 34:1–2 says the second tables did not contain the higher priesthood ordinances, which the Lord took away because of rebellion, leaving the lesser law (D&C 84:19–27).","Abinadi’s face shone “as Moses’ did while in the mount of Sinai” (Mosiah 13:5). The Hebrew word for “shone” is related to the word for “horn,” which led to old paintings and statues of Moses with horns."]},
"exo.35":{title:"Whosoever is of a willing heart",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses gives the Sabbath law—He invites offerings from all who are of a willing heart—Men and women bring gold, jewels, linen and skins, and wise-hearted women spin—Bezaleel and Aholiab are called and given skill to teach others.",
 bg:["Chapters 35–40 repeat the instructions of chapters 25–31, now showing them carried out. The repetition stresses that Israel did exactly what the Lord asked.","Women contributed their jewelry and their skill (35:22–26). Building the Lord’s house was the work of the whole people."]},
"exo.36":{title:"Much more than enough",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The skilled workers begin the sanctuary—The people bring so much that Moses must restrain them from giving—The curtains, coverings, boards, bars and vail of the tabernacle are made.",
 bg:["This is one of the few times in scripture people had to be told to stop giving (36:6–7). It shows how fully their hearts had turned after the golden calf.","The tabernacle’s fabrics used blue, purple and scarlet dyes, which were rare and costly in the ancient world."]},
"exo.37":{title:"Bezaleel made the ark",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Bezaleel makes the ark of shittim wood overlaid with gold—He makes the mercy seat with two cherubims of beaten gold—He makes the table of shewbread, the golden candlestick with seven lamps and the altar of incense.",
 bg:["The cherubims faced each other with wings spread over the mercy seat (37:9). Cherubim also guarded the way to the tree of life in Eden (Genesis 3:24).","The shewbread, twelve loaves set before the Lord each Sabbath (Leviticus 24:5–8), was a constant reminder that the Lord provides daily bread."]},
"exo.38":{title:"The laver of brass",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Bezaleel makes the altar of burnt offering and the laver of brass from the women’s mirrors—The court and its hangings are made—The gold, silver and brass are counted, the silver coming from the half shekel of 603,550 men.",
 bg:["Mirrors in the ancient world were made of polished bronze, so the women gave up valued possessions for the laver (38:8).","The careful accounting of the metals (38:21–31) showed that the people’s offerings were used honestly for the Lord’s house."]},
"exo.39":{title:"As the LORD commanded Moses",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The holy garments are made: the ephod, the breastplate with twelve stones, the robe, coats, mitre and the gold plate HOLINESS TO THE LORD—The work of the tabernacle is finished—Moses sees they have done all as the Lord commanded, and he blesses them.",
 bg:["The phrase “as the LORD commanded Moses” appears seven times in this chapter. Exact obedience to the pattern mattered because the tabernacle taught about the Lord.","When the work was finished, Moses inspected it and “blessed them” (39:43), much as God saw his creation and called it good."]},
"exo.40":{title:"The glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"On the first day of the first month of the second year, the tabernacle is set up—The furnishings are placed, and Aaron and his sons are washed, anointed and clothed—A cloud covers the tent, and the glory of the Lord fills the tabernacle—The cloud guides Israel on all their journeys.",
 bg:["The tabernacle was set up about a year after Israel left Egypt (40:17). A cloud filled Solomon’s temple in the same way when it was dedicated (1 Kings 8:10–11).","Exodus begins with Israel in bondage and ends with the Lord dwelling among his people. Temples today serve the same purpose: a place where God may dwell with his people."]},
});
